      "description": "Watch Hungary vs Ukraine highlights and goals from UEFA Nations League on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on September 25, 2026. The UEFA Nations League brought together two determined sides on September 25, 2026 , as Hungary hosted Ukraine in a clash filled with tactical intrigue and high stakes. Both teams looked to assert their dominance early in a match where every inch of the pitch was fiercely contested. Hungary Frustrated by Woodwork and Goal-Line Heroics The home side started with immense purpose, creating the first real opening after just 12 minutes. Daniel Lukacs unleashed a thunderous volley that beat the keeper but rattled back off the crossbar, much to the dismay of the home crowd. Hungary continued to press, and they thought they had found the breakthrough when Milos Kerkez fired toward goal, only to see his effort hacked off the line by a desperate Ukrainian defense. Despite the pressure from the hosts, Ukraine remained patient and clinical. In the 42nd minute, a sweeping move orchestrated by Mykola Matviyenko saw Georgiy Sudakov deliver a pinpoint cross into the heart of the box. Danylo Sikan rose highest, guiding a superb header into the net to give the visitors the lead against the run of play. It was a hammer blow for the Hungarians just before the interval. Late Dismissal and Stoppage Time Chaos The second half saw Hungary dominate possession, reaching 56% as they searched for an equalizer. Tactical substitutions followed, with Donat Barany and Gabor Jurek introduced to spark the attack. However, Ukraine 's goalkeeper Dmytro Riznyk stood firm, comfortably dealing with a dangerous header from Kerkez in the 87th minute. The defensive discipline of the visitors kept the Hungarian frontline at bay as the clock ticked down. The match reached a boiling point in stoppage time. Oleksandr Nazarenko , having already been booked, received a second yellow card in the 92nd minute for unsportsmanlike conduct and was sent off. The atmosphere turned volatile two minutes later as a mass confrontation led to four yellow cards being brandished simultaneously to Dominik Szoboszlai , Milos Kerkez , Viktor Tsigankov , and Matviyenko . Despite the numerical advantage for the final seconds, Hungary could not find a way through, and Ukraine secured a hard-fought victory."
